WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:12.760
Film Courage: Going back to before you applied
to film school and what you thought was your

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.359
vision of this is where I am going to be the
next Cassavetes or whatever?

00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:22.859
And then time moves on and was it in line
with that?

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:27.260
Because people think it’s going to be this
and I’m going to be driving in a BMW down

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:28.260
the 101.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.260
Mark Sanderson, author/screenwriter: No, it
never is.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.330
The romanticized image, no.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.960
It’s more of a craftsperson job.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.567
It’s a career but I look at it as a job
and I’m blessed to have the job.

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:41.280
It is my job and I’m blessed to have the
job and I’m blessed to be working.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.490
I really like to work and I take as much work
as I can and turn down things that I don’t

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:50.050
like or can’t do but it’s nice to be in
a place where a producer calls you and says

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.050
“Are you available?”

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:56.210
I’ve also been in a place where you turn
in a thing and you say “Am I good enough?”

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.710
And you wait 6 months later and they say “I
didn’t read it yet.”

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.800
Which is the norm.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:10.350
So whatever level of the business you are
working at to have any type of success (I’ve

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:14.170
realized is an amazing thing).

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:19.170
And many people can look down at (unless you’re
the highest echelon at the A-list) and say

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.780
well if you’re not that, I’m not impress
by what you do.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.170
And so I say “Okay, I get it.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.470
You’ve never really struggled to get one
thing made so you wouldn’t say that to someone

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:32.230
who has been slogging away, the 10-year-overnight-success.”

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:36.260
Like I said it was 6 years out of film school
(6 years) before I had my first professional

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.590
job that I was later fired from quickly.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:44.530
And then 7 years for my spec to go through
all the wrong people and the spec almost won

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.470
the Nichol Fellowship and all these adventures
with that one spec.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:54.190
I wrote other ones already for me to finally
end up with me sitting on the set during the

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.190
filming going “Wow!

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.140
This has been 7 years?”

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:03.560
And every script and project has it’s own
journey but sure out of film school I thought

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:07.020
they were going to hand us our diploma and
our three-picture deal (which is the joke)

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.410
which is like “Of course I’ll be working.”

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.200
Maybe as a PA which I never did.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.610
I knew how to make films I didn’t want to
spend 18 hours a day not writing.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:23.531
So I worked in restaurants as a waiter because
I could always write, you know that’s me

00:02:23.531 --> 00:02:26.090
going through college.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.581
But then you settle into a place where you
are finally at whatever at level you are and

00:02:30.581 --> 00:02:34.820
have the players that you hire and the people
that you know.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.750
But do I still want to get to higher levels,
absolutely.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:44.700
But no one can take away my ability to write
and create projects.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.913
So that’s thing so that’s the next step
would be for me to move into the producing

00:02:47.913 --> 00:02:48.913
aspect and create projects.

00:02:48.913 --> 00:02:49.913
So that’s the thing.

00:02:49.913 --> 00:02:51.470
So the next step would be for me moving into
a more producing aspect.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:57.110
Directing I’ve enjoyed but I probably would
have pursued it out of film school.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:01.700
I really like the fact that as a writer you
can go anyone.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.069
Like I mentioned I was in Italy last year
for 6 weeks and I was writing a screenplay.

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:11.380
So if you have a laptop and Internet connection
you can write anywhere but a director needs

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.780
a film crew.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:20.750
I have friends who can work two years on a
movie directing it from beginning to end (it’s

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:21.750
a big endeavor).

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:31.010
I like the fact of being able to be mobile
and work and maybe do more.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.349
This last year I had 6 movies written.

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:36.570
So I don’t think I could direct 6 movies
a year.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.770
Film Courage: Yeah, it’s just so expensive.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:46.069
That’s great thing about the writing, even
though sometimes they [the writer] is unappreciated,

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:52.540
it’s the heartbeat of the movie and you
can be on vacation while you are doing something

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.560
else like writing.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:01.030
But in terms of you grew up in Southern California,
so I wonder does that taint people and not

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:05.480
in a bad way but in terms of the way the vision
of the way a career looks like?

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.540
Versus someone that is in another state?

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:13.640
Mark: Yeah, it’s weird because it never
seemed weird when my friend and I were making

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.639
movies as a kid it never seemed like such
a weird thing because we were filmmakers because

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:21.169
they were filming movies on our block outside.

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:23.550
The biggest TV shows were right up the block,
you know?

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.210
Santa Monica used to film everything there.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:27.460
So we were like “Oh?”

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:33.540
Now if we were living in a small city some
where it might be so impressive or “I could

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.979
never make a film because it’s such a big
enormous dream.”

00:04:36.979 --> 00:04:43.610
But it was right outside our door so we were
like “Huh?”

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.830
In a good way or bad because you don’t know
if you’re ever going to be able to do it

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.639
and achieve and sustain that.

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:57.509
So I think the wide-eyed dreamer is still
inside and I need that as a kid but then you

00:04:57.509 --> 00:05:01.710
grow up and you realize the realities of the
business.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:12.229
Who wants to spend millions of dollars and
lose their money on their idea.

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:19.759
You know it’s a business unfortunately but
if you can work in it as artists.

00:05:19.759 --> 00:05:24.020
And some are blessed to be an “artiste,”
an “auteur.”

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.449
And others are people who get to work.

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:31.809
And if I’m working in the film business
I’m a happy person.

00:05:31.809 --> 00:05:37.009
Film Courage: Do you think you realize the
amount of time spent alone at a computer or

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:38.009
at a desk?

00:05:38.009 --> 00:05:41.539
I mean I realize you can be in a cafe writing
and for a lot of people that does work.

00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:46.869
But do you think a lot of people realize being
bound by this calendar?

00:05:46.869 --> 00:05:52.210
The calendar that says “Okay, I need this
done by this date and I can’t deviate from

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:53.210
that.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:57.469
Mark: Well in school or college you have a
report or whatever do.

00:05:57.469 --> 00:06:04.639
There are do dates for everything but that
made it seem more like a job.

00:06:04.639 --> 00:06:08.240
Which in the past it seemed more romanticized
like “I’ll sell my specs, I sell it and

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:12.509
there will be some weird thing that happens
where you won’t have to work so hard because

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:13.509
you did the work, right?

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:17.879
You sold spec with all the rewrites and the
possibilities that can happen.

00:06:17.879 --> 00:06:20.369
Then when you start working in the business
you start realizing all the possibilities

00:06:20.369 --> 00:06:22.289
that do happen and you’re like “Oh wow?”

00:06:22.289 --> 00:06:28.849
And it sort of knocks down the shimmer of
this romanticized driving with the top down…I

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:29.849
had a convertible one time…

00:06:29.849 --> 00:06:32.780
Film Courage: [Laughs] I don’t know why
that came into my head?

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:38.339
Mark: No, it was a 1965 Buick which was beautiful
and I would drive up the coast.

00:06:38.339 --> 00:06:44.069
And you know driving on the lot and all this
stuff you dream of but then you still have

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:49.659
to do the work and like I said I worked with
a lot of “Actors” back in the restaurant

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:51.990
days who didn’t want to do the work.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.179
They loved you know [takes an imaginary drag
off of a cigarette and exhales] “I’m an

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:54.179
actor.”

00:06:54.179 --> 00:06:55.179
Brooding and this.

00:06:55.179 --> 00:06:56.179
“Are you taking classes?”

00:06:56.179 --> 00:06:57.179
“Well no?

00:06:57.179 --> 00:06:58.649
I went on this audition because I have a good
look.”

00:06:58.649 --> 00:07:01.449
And I’m like “Oh, so you just don’t
want to do the work?

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:06.789
Or you think it’s just that easy?”

00:07:06.789 --> 00:07:11.460
So yeah, it’s changed, the perception I
think off once you work at something where

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.809
you have this outside perception that many
people might have of “How amazing it is

00:07:15.809 --> 00:07:16.809
to be a writer!”

00:07:16.809 --> 00:07:21.870
Yeah, it is but as a writer take the Hollywood
element out, it’s a grind.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:23.179
You’ve got to write.

00:07:23.179 --> 00:07:25.499
You’ve got to come up with stuff.

00:07:25.499 --> 00:07:32.419
35 scripts, I’m like “How did I channel
that many stories through this body into these

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:33.419
things?”

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:38.289
And then I go “There won’t be any others
in the well, how could there be?”

00:07:38.289 --> 00:07:40.110
Well that’s your job as a writer, right?

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.309
To come up with another story, to come up
with another, right?

00:07:42.309 --> 00:07:46.580
I mean that’s the beautiful part of it to
come back to the well and have it be full

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:53.300
of stuff and that’s where your living comes
into play, your experiences and living an

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.930
authentic life where you are always observing
and doing things because it’s your homework

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:59.849
as a screenwriter to do that.

00:07:59.849 --> 00:08:02.680
Because you don’t want to go back to your
well and have it be empty.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.699
That’s when your passion dries up and you’re
like “I’ve got nothing to say.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:08.699
What am I doing?”

00:08:08.699 --> 00:08:11.029
And you shrink and you just disappear.

00:08:11.029 --> 00:08:15.509
And I think while you’re trying to pursue
your career you have to live an interesting

00:08:15.509 --> 00:08:23.899
life and meet interesting people and have
it all relate back to your screenwriting because

00:08:23.899 --> 00:08:24.899
it does.

00:08:24.899 --> 00:08:29.780
It’s your job to live an interesting, authentic
life and I think that helps your work.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:29.949
Question For The Viewers: Is your Hollywood
career unfolding the way you imagined it would?

